--   Lemma 3.2 in the extended non-negative reals
-- statement:
--   **Lemma 3.2 of Bux–Kassmann–Schulze**, transcribed into $[0,\infty]$. For any sets $V, W$
--   and any $s \in A_1(x)$, $t \in A_1(y)$ — the full open unit cubes at $x$ and $y$ —
--
--   $$\mathbf 1_{V_{\sqrt d/2}[x]}(t) + \mathbf 1_{W_{\sqrt d/2}[y]}(s)
--   \;\ge\;
--   \mathbf 1_{V_{\sqrt d}[x]}(y) + \mathbf 1_{W_{\sqrt d}[y]}(x)$$
--
--   with all four indicators valued in `ℝ≥0∞`, where $V_r = \{u \in V : \bar B(u,r) \subseteq V\}$
--   is the shrinking of Definition 2.1 and $V[x] = V + x$.
--
--   This is the form Proposition 3.5's estimate consumes. The kernel bounds (2) live in
--   $[0,\infty]$ because $k$ is allowed the value $\infty$, so the integral estimate is carried out
--   there throughout; a real-valued inequality would have to be transported across the coercion at
--   every step. `QFS.lemma_min_dist` is the same statement with real-valued indicators, and
--   `QFS.lemma_min_dist_favoured` is the source's own hypotheses — reference cones at favoured
--   indices, with $s, t$ confined to the cone-cut parts of the cubes. All three are `Proved`.
--
--   As in the real-valued form, none of the source's lattice or favouring hypotheses is needed: the
--   argument uses only that $A_1(y) \subseteq B_{\sqrt d/2}(y)$.
